{"id":1187,"date":"2026-07-01T12:55:52","date_gmt":"2026-07-01T12:55:52","guid":{"rendered":"https:\/\/jiushihema.github.io\/pls_lab\/?p=1187"},"modified":"2026-07-17T08:18:13","modified_gmt":"2026-07-17T08:18:13","slug":"%e9%9d%a2%e5%90%91-simo-%e5%9c%ba%e6%99%af%e4%bd%8e%e6%97%b6%e5%bb%b6-5g-%e8%ae%be%e5%a4%87%e8%af%86%e5%88%ab%e7%9a%84%e4%bf%a1%e9%81%93%e9%b2%81%e6%a3%92%e5%b0%84%e9%a2%91%e6%8c%87%e7%ba%b9","status":"publish","type":"post","link":"https:\/\/jiushihema.github.io\/pls_lab\/en\/%e9%9d%a2%e5%90%91-simo-%e5%9c%ba%e6%99%af%e4%bd%8e%e6%97%b6%e5%bb%b6-5g-%e8%ae%be%e5%a4%87%e8%af%86%e5%88%ab%e7%9a%84%e4%bf%a1%e9%81%93%e9%b2%81%e6%a3%92%e5%b0%84%e9%a2%91%e6%8c%87%e7%ba%b9\/","title":{"rendered":"Channel-Robust RF Fingerprinting Extraction Method for Low-Latency 5G Device Identification in SIMO Scenarios"},"content":{"rendered":"<div class=\"wp-block-group has-global-padding is-layout-constrained wp-container-core-group-is-layout-3749991f wp-block-group-is-layout-constrained\" style=\"min-height:1000px\">\n<div class=\"wp-block-group is-vertical is-content-justification-center is-layout-flex wp-container-core-group-is-layout-458a08b5 wp-block-group-is-layout-flex\"><h2 class=\"lab-paper-title wp-block-post-title\">Channel-Robust RF Fingerprinting Extraction Method for Low-Latency 5G Device Identification in SIMO Scenarios<\/h2>\n\n\n<p class=\"lab-paper-meta has-small-font-size wp-block-paragraph\">Yingjie Sun and Guyue Li and Hongfu Chou and Aiqun Hu<\/p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"lab-paper-meta has-small-font-size wp-block-paragraph\"><em>2026 IEEE Wireless Communications and Networking Conference (WCNC)<\/em><\/p>\n<\/div>\n\n\n\n<hr class=\"wp-block-separator has-text-color has-contrast-3-color has-alpha-channel-opacity has-contrast-3-background-color has-background is-style-wide\" style=\"margin-top:var(--wp--preset--spacing--20);margin-bottom:var(--wp--preset--spacing--20)\"\/>\n\n\n\n<div class=\"wp-block-group is-layout-flow wp-block-group-is-layout-flow\">\n<div class=\"wp-block-uagb-advanced-heading uagb-block-46005010 lab-subheading\"><h3 class=\"uagb-heading-text\">\ud83d\udccc Abstract<\/h3><\/div>\n\n\n\n<figure class=\"wp-block-pullquote has-text-align-left lab-abstract-quote has-small-font-size\" style=\"margin-right:var(--wp--preset--spacing--10);margin-left:var(--wp--preset--spacing--10);padding-top:0;padding-right:0;padding-bottom:0;padding-left:0\"><blockquote><p>Ultra-low latency is a hallmark feature of the fifth-generation mobile communications (5G), imposing extremely stringent time requirements on device identification. Current cryptography-based schemes introduce additional computational overhead, thereby exacerbating identification latency. Radio frequency fingerprinting (RFF) identifies devices at the physical layer, which can prevent impersonation attacks and significantly reduce latency. However, multipath channels impair the accuracy of RFF, and existing channel-robust methods require feedback or processing across multiple time instants, incurring additional signaling latency. To address this issue, this paper proposes a novel RFF extraction technique that leverages signals from multiple receive antennas to mitigate multipath effects without increasing latency. Unlike single-domain methods, this work utilizes the Log-Linear Delta Ratio (LLDR) of simultaneous channel frequency responses (CFR) from multiple antennas to preserve discriminative RFF features, thereby eliminating multi-instant sampling and shortening acquisition time. To overcome the challenge of relying on minimal channel variation, the frequency band is divided into subbands, and the LLDR is computed independently within each subband. Simulation results show that in a 20-tap channel at a 20 dB signal-to-noise ratio (SNR), the proposed scheme achieves 96.13% identification accuracy for 30 user equipments (UEs). Furthermore, the theoretical latency evaluated based on the Roofline model yields an over-the-air latency of 0.491 ms, satisfying the latency requirements of ultra-reliable low-latency communication (URLLC).<\/p><\/blockquote><\/figure>\n\n\n\n<div class=\"wp-block-uagb-advanced-heading uagb-block-6adec98e lab-subheading\"><h3 class=\"uagb-heading-text\">\ud83d\udca1 Key Contributions<\/h3><\/div>\n\n\n\n<ul start=\"1\" style=\"border-style:none;border-width:0px\" class=\"wp-block-list lab-feature-list\">\n<li class=\"lab-feature-list\"><strong>Proposes a novel RFF scheme utilizing multi-antenna received signals<\/strong>: Only a single uplink transmission is required to mitigate multipath channel effects, thereby reducing identification latency, and the method can be theoretically proven to be robust against multipath channels.<\/li>\n\n\n\n<li class=\"lab-feature-list\"><strong>Computes the Log-Linear Delta Ratio (LLDR) between CFRs<\/strong>: Distinct from traditional single-domain signal processing; to address CFR fluctuations, the frequency band is divided and RFF features are extracted within each smooth subband.<\/li>\n\n\n\n<li class=\"lab-feature-list\"><strong>Simulation validation based on MATLAB 5G NR Toolbox<\/strong>: Simulations are conducted for 30 virtual UEs under different SNRs, and results show that in a TDL 20-tap channel at an SNR of 20 dB, an accuracy of 96.13% and an over-the-air latency of 0.491 ms are achieved, meeting URLLC latency requirements.<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n\n\n\n<div class=\"wp-block-uagb-advanced-heading uagb-block-2f9d57f4 lab-subheading\"><h3 class=\"uagb-heading-text\">\ud83d\udd27 Method Overview<\/h3><\/div>\n\n\n\n<div class=\"wp-block-group lab-paper-results is-nowrap is-layout-flex wp-container-core-group-is-layout-83d1bd77 wp-block-group-is-layout-flex\" style=\"margin-top:0;margin-bottom:0;padding-right:0;padding-left:0\">\n<div class=\"wp-block-group is-vertical is-layout-flex wp-container-core-group-is-layout-8da03e20 wp-block-group-is-layout-flex\" style=\"margin-top:0;margin-bottom:0;padding-top:0;padding-right:0;padding-bottom:0;padding-left:0\">\n<div class=\"wp-block-uagb-image uagb-block-db58d614 wp-block-uagb-image--layout-default wp-block-uagb-image--effect-static wp-block-uagb-image--align-none wp-container-content-9cfa9a5a\"><figure class=\"wp-block-uagb-image__figure\"><img decoding=\"async\" srcset=\"https:\/\/jiushihema.github.io\/pls_lab\/wp-content\/uploads\/2026\/07\/77080c0d-bf4d-452d-8783-db1e8d8486ad.png ,https:\/\/jiushihema.github.io\/pls_lab\/wp-content\/uploads\/2026\/07\/77080c0d-bf4d-452d-8783-db1e8d8486ad.png 780w, https:\/\/jiushihema.github.io\/pls_lab\/wp-content\/uploads\/2026\/07\/77080c0d-bf4d-452d-8783-db1e8d8486ad.png 360w\" sizes=\"auto, (max-width: 480px) 150px\" src=\"https:\/\/jiushihema.github.io\/pls_lab\/wp-content\/uploads\/2026\/07\/77080c0d-bf4d-452d-8783-db1e8d8486ad.png\" alt=\"\" class=\"uag-image-1189\" width=\"295\" height=\"230\" title=\"77080c0d-bf4d-452d-8783-db1e8d8486ad\" loading=\"lazy\" role=\"img\"\/><\/figure><\/div>\n<\/div>\n\n\n\n<div class=\"wp-block-group is-vertical is-layout-flex wp-container-core-group-is-layout-831b2db5 wp-block-group-is-layout-flex\">\n<p class=\"lab-experiment-caption has-medium-font-size wp-block-paragraph\"><strong>System and signal model:<\/strong>In a SIMO system, the frequency-domain received signals at two receive antennas are $Y_1(k)=R_1(k)H_1(k)T(k)X(k)+N_1(k)$, $Y_2(k)=R_2(k)H_2(k)T(k)X(k)+N_2(k)$, where $T(k)$ denotes the transmit impairment, $R_1(k),R_2(k)$ denote the receive impairments, and $H_1(k),H_2(k)$ denote the CFR. The channel terms superimposed in the channel estimation results $Z_1(k)\\approx R_1(k)H_1(k)T(k)$, $Z_2(k)\\approx R_2(k)H_2(k)T(k)$ hinder the direct acquisition of stable RFF.<\/p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"lab-experiment-caption has-small-font-size wp-block-paragraph\"><strong>LLDR extraction and subband processing:<\/strong>The frequency band is divided into subbands to ensure CFR smoothness, and normalization is performed within each subband using the means $\\hat{a}_1,\\hat{a}_2$. Defining the relative impairment between antennas as $r=\\frac{R_2(k)}{R_1(k)}$, the linear difference $D_{12}(k)=Z_{1,norm}(k)-\\frac{1}{r}Z_{2,norm}(k)$ and the logarithmic difference $L_{12}(k)=\\ln r+\\ln\\frac{Z_{1,norm}(k)}{Z_{2,norm}(k)}$ are constructed. Based on the first-order Taylor approximation $\\ln x\\approx\\Delta x$, the ratio of the two yields the RFF estimate $\\hat{T}_0(k)=\\frac{D_{12}(k)}{L_{12}(k)}\\approx T_0(k)$, which is independent of $H_1(k),H_2(k)$, achieving multipath suppression; repeating per subband and concatenating yields the channel-robust RFF, which is then fed into a CNN for identification.<\/p>\n<\/div>\n<\/div>\n\n\n\n<div class=\"wp-block-uagb-advanced-heading uagb-block-46951038 lab-subheading\"><h3 class=\"uagb-heading-text\">\ud83d\udcca Experimental Results<\/h3><\/div>\n\n\n\n<div class=\"wp-block-group lab-paper-results is-nowrap is-layout-flex wp-container-core-group-is-layout-3a88641f wp-block-group-is-layout-flex\">\n<div class=\"wp-block-group is-vertical is-layout-flex wp-container-core-group-is-layout-831b2db5 wp-block-group-is-layout-flex\">\n<div class=\"wp-block-group is-vertical is-layout-flex wp-container-core-group-is-layout-831b2db5 wp-block-group-is-layout-flex\">\n<div class=\"wp-block-uagb-image uagb-block-a3591eb7 wp-block-uagb-image--layout-default wp-block-uagb-image--effect-static wp-block-uagb-image--align-none\"><figure class=\"wp-block-uagb-image__figure\"><img decoding=\"async\" srcset=\"https:\/\/jiushihema.github.io\/pls_lab\/wp-content\/uploads\/2026\/07\/746a3f74-370f-49f7-a1f3-45b4ee534733-1.png ,https:\/\/jiushihema.github.io\/pls_lab\/wp-content\/uploads\/2026\/07\/746a3f74-370f-49f7-a1f3-45b4ee534733-1.png 780w, https:\/\/jiushihema.github.io\/pls_lab\/wp-content\/uploads\/2026\/07\/746a3f74-370f-49f7-a1f3-45b4ee534733-1.png 360w\" sizes=\"auto, (max-width: 480px) 150px\" src=\"https:\/\/jiushihema.github.io\/pls_lab\/wp-content\/uploads\/2026\/07\/746a3f74-370f-49f7-a1f3-45b4ee534733-1.png\" alt=\"\" class=\"uag-image-1204\" width=\"760\" height=\"588\" title=\"746a3f74-370f-49f7-a1f3-45b4ee534733\" loading=\"lazy\" role=\"img\"\/><\/figure><\/div>\n\n\n\n<div class=\"wp-block-uagb-image uagb-block-14bb0b9c wp-block-uagb-image--layout-default wp-block-uagb-image--effect-static wp-block-uagb-image--align-none wp-container-content-9cfa9a5a\"><figure class=\"wp-block-uagb-image__figure\"><img decoding=\"async\" srcset=\"https:\/\/jiushihema.github.io\/pls_lab\/wp-content\/uploads\/2026\/07\/158aa649-6565-4efa-a707-3d26f4ee076f.png ,https:\/\/jiushihema.github.io\/pls_lab\/wp-content\/uploads\/2026\/07\/158aa649-6565-4efa-a707-3d26f4ee076f.png 780w, https:\/\/jiushihema.github.io\/pls_lab\/wp-content\/uploads\/2026\/07\/158aa649-6565-4efa-a707-3d26f4ee076f.png 360w\" sizes=\"auto, (max-width: 480px) 150px\" src=\"https:\/\/jiushihema.github.io\/pls_lab\/wp-content\/uploads\/2026\/07\/158aa649-6565-4efa-a707-3d26f4ee076f.png\" alt=\"\" class=\"uag-image-1190\" width=\"802\" height=\"656\" title=\"158aa649-6565-4efa-a707-3d26f4ee076f\" loading=\"lazy\" role=\"img\"\/><\/figure><\/div>\n<\/div>\n<\/div>\n\n\n\n<div class=\"wp-block-group is-vertical is-layout-flex wp-container-core-group-is-layout-831b2db5 wp-block-group-is-layout-flex\">\n<div class=\"wp-block-uagb-image uagb-block-f74d106b wp-block-uagb-image--layout-default wp-block-uagb-image--effect-static wp-block-uagb-image--align-none\"><figure class=\"wp-block-uagb-image__figure\"><img decoding=\"async\" srcset=\"https:\/\/jiushihema.github.io\/pls_lab\/wp-content\/uploads\/2026\/07\/9e6c75e7-ce1b-41f6-8930-f5f766302353.png ,https:\/\/jiushihema.github.io\/pls_lab\/wp-content\/uploads\/2026\/07\/9e6c75e7-ce1b-41f6-8930-f5f766302353.png 780w, https:\/\/jiushihema.github.io\/pls_lab\/wp-content\/uploads\/2026\/07\/9e6c75e7-ce1b-41f6-8930-f5f766302353.png 360w\" sizes=\"auto, (max-width: 480px) 150px\" src=\"https:\/\/jiushihema.github.io\/pls_lab\/wp-content\/uploads\/2026\/07\/9e6c75e7-ce1b-41f6-8930-f5f766302353.png\" alt=\"\" class=\"uag-image-1205\" width=\"775\" height=\"652\" title=\"9e6c75e7-ce1b-41f6-8930-f5f766302353\" loading=\"lazy\" role=\"img\"\/><\/figure><\/div>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"lab-experiment-caption has-medium-font-size wp-block-paragraph\"><strong>The proposed scheme significantly outperforms all baseline methods under multipath channels while meeting URLLC latency requirements<\/strong>: As shown in Fig. 7, at 25 dB SNR, the proposed method achieves 96.2% accuracy, outperforming all baselines, with Benchmark1 at 61.83%, Benchmark2 at 62.4%, and DoLoS at 83.67%. Furthermore, Fig. 5 shows that with 4 subcarriers and SNR above 20 dB, an accuracy of 96.43% is achieved; Fig. 6 shows that with 30 UEs and SNR greater than 10 dB, accuracy can reach above 95.4%, and with fewer than 20 UEs, it can reach 98.2%. In terms of latency, the estimates are $T_{UE}=34.64\\,\\mu s$, $T_{RFFI}=81.04\\,\\mu s$, combined with $T_{TTI}=0.25\\,ms$, $T_f=0.125\\,ms$, yielding an overall theoretical latency of $T_{air}\\approx 0.491\\,ms&lt;1\\,ms$, satisfying the URLLC requirements of ITU-R IMT-2020.<\/p>\n<\/div>\n<\/div>\n\n\n\n<div class=\"wp-block-uagb-advanced-heading uagb-block-e3c2d50d lab-subheading\"><h3 class=\"uagb-heading-text\">\ud83d\udcdd Cite This Paper<\/h3><\/div>\n\n\n\n<pre class=\"wp-block-code lab-bibtex-code has-small-font-size\"><code>@inproceedings{sun2026channelrobust,\n  author    = {Yingjie Sun and Guyue Li and Hongfu Chou and Aiqun Hu},\n  title     = {Channel-Robust RFF for Low-Latency 5G Device Identification in SIMO Scenarios},\n  booktitle = {2026 IEEE Wireless Communications and Networking Conference (WCNC)},\n  year      = {2026}\n}\n<\/code><\/pre>\n<\/div>\n\n\n\n<hr class=\"wp-block-separator has-alpha-channel-opacity is-style-wide\" style=\"margin-top:var(--wp--preset--spacing--20);margin-bottom:var(--wp--preset--spacing--20)\"\/>\n\n\n\n<div class=\"wp-block-group is-layout-flow wp-block-group-is-layout-flow\">\n<div style=\"height:100